1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:10,000
Which high-impact papers are worth replicating —
and has anyone actually checked them?

2
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:16,000
The OpenAIRE Graph can't tell you.
Replication Radar can.

3
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:27,000
Search a field, and it ranks
what's worth re-testing…

4
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:36,000
…then overlays, live, which claims were
independently checked — and how they held.

5
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:48,000
This one was checked: validated,
traceable to a signed nanopublication.

6
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:57,000
That verification layer is what
a citation graph can't hold.

7
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:13,000
That's the human view. Now the same engine
as an MCP — driven by an AI agent.

8
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:28,000
The agent asks the Radar what's un-replicated.
Top hit: a Nature marine-heatwave paper.

9
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:36,000
Highly cited — never independently checked.

10
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:50,000
Which software to reuse? It ranks real tools
by reuse signal and picks XMHW — independent.

11
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:58,000
It even flags one that looks independent
but is the author's own code.

12
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:18,000
For data, it hands off to the OpenAIRE MCP —
an independent satellite dataset.

13
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:24,000
A different producer from the original.
A true replication, not a re-run.

14
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:35,000
And it's honest: satellite data only reaches the
1980s — the century-long trend stays an open question.

15
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:46,000
It scaffolds the repo from the FORRT template
and records the paper, data, and software.

16
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:49,500
Two MCPs on one Graph — discover, then check.
That's Replication Radar.
